<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">

  <!--
    OWASP dependency-check suppressions.

    A suppression is an assertion that a reported CVE does not apply to THIS application.
    It is a security judgement, not a way to quiet the build, so every entry must record
    who decided and why. An undocumented suppression is worse than the finding: it hides
    a real risk behind the appearance of a clean report.

    Only suppress after triage establishes one of:
      1. FALSE POSITIVE  - dependency-check matched the wrong product or version.
                           Check "CPE Confidence" in the report; LOW/MEDIUM is suspect.
      2. NOT REACHABLE   - the vulnerable code path cannot be reached in this app, e.g.
                           the feature is disabled, or the input is never attacker
                           controlled. State the assumption, because assumptions expire.
      3. NO FIX + ACCEPTED - no patched version exists and the risk is consciously taken.
                           Prefer <until> so it comes back for review.

    If a fixed version exists, upgrade instead. Do not suppress.

    Regenerate the report with:  ./mvnw dependency-check:check -Dformat=ALL
    The CSV output is the practical triage worklist.

    ===========================================================================
    Triaged 2026-07-24 (Jason McKay). The build gates at CVSS >= 7 (see the
    dependency-check-maven config in pom.xml). Dispositions:
      - netty (3x 7.5 HIGH, STOMP/SPDY codec DoS) -> FIXED, not suppressed:
        netty.version pinned to 4.1.136.Final in pom.xml.
      - log4j-api           -> NOT APPLICABLE (below).
      - swagger-ui/DOMPurify -> NOT REACHABLE in prod (below).
      - jackson-databind    -> NO FIX + ACCEPTED, below the gate (below).
    ===========================================================================
  -->

  <!--
    log4j-api 2.24.3: CVE-2026-34477, CVE-2026-34479, CVE-2026-49844.
    Decision: NOT APPLICABLE (wrong artifact).
    Reason:   These CVEs are in log4j-core and the log4j-1.2-api bridge
              (e.g. CVE-2026-34479 is the Log4j1XmlLayout output-escaping flaw,
              fixed in log4j 2.25.4). This app ships only log4j-api (the SLF4J
              facade) and log4j-to-slf4j (the bridge to Logback); both are flagged
              because they share the apache:log4j CPE. `mvn dependency:tree`
              confirms neither log4j-core nor log4j-1.2-api is on the classpath,
              and no Log4j layout is configured. The vulnerable code cannot run.
    Assumes:  logging stays SLF4J/Logback via log4j-to-slf4j; log4j-core and
              log4j-1.2-api never get added. Listed by specific CVE on purpose, so
              a genuine future log4j-api/to-slf4j CVE is NOT auto-suppressed.
    Reviewed: Jason McKay, 2026-07-24
  -->
  <suppress>
    <filePath regex="true">.*[/\\]log4j-(api|to-slf4j)-.*\.jar</filePath>
    <cve>CVE-2026-34477</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-34479</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-49844</cve>
  </suppress>

  <!--
    swagger-ui 5.32.2 bundled JS: DOMPurify 3.3.2 (swagger-ui-bundle.js and
    swagger-ui-es-bundle.js). Multiple RetireJS findings (CVE-2026-41238/41239/
    41240/49458/49459/49978/65898-65903, GHSA-c2j3-45gr-mqc4), mostly XSS-class.
    Decision: NOT REACHABLE in production.
    Reason:   These are client-side XSS/sanitiser-bypass issues in the DOMPurify
              copy bundled inside Swagger UI. Swagger UI (and the OpenAPI docs) are
              served only under the `dev` profile: the base config sets
              springdoc.api-docs.enabled=false / springdoc.swagger-ui.enabled=false,
              re-enabled only in application-dev.properties (pinned by
              SpringdocExposureTest). In any non-dev deployment both routes 404, so
              the bundled DOMPurify never loads in a browser. Scoped to the bundled
              DOMPurify package because the same "not served in prod" reasoning
              covers every finding in that bundle equally.
    Assumes:  Swagger UI is never enabled in a production/internet-facing profile.
              If that changes, remove this block and upgrade springdoc/swagger-ui.
    Reviewed: Jason McKay, 2026-07-24
  -->
  <suppress>
    <filePath regex="true">.*swagger-ui-.*\.jar.*</filePath>
    <vulnerabilityName>CVE-2026-41238</vulnerabilityName>
    <vulnerabilityName>CVE-2026-41239</vulnerabilityName>
    <vulnerabilityName>CVE-2026-41240</vulnerabilityName>
    <vulnerabilityName>CVE-2026-49458</vulnerabilityName>
    <vulnerabilityName>CVE-2026-49459</vulnerabilityName>
    <vulnerabilityName>CVE-2026-49978</vulnerabilityName>
    <vulnerabilityName>CVE-2026-65898</vulnerabilityName>
    <vulnerabilityName>CVE-2026-65899</vulnerabilityName>
    <vulnerabilityName>CVE-2026-65900</vulnerabilityName>
    <vulnerabilityName>CVE-2026-65901</vulnerabilityName>
    <vulnerabilityName>CVE-2026-65902</vulnerabilityName>
    <vulnerabilityName>CVE-2026-65903</vulnerabilityName>
    <vulnerabilityName>GHSA-c2j3-45gr-mqc4</vulnerabilityName>
  </suppress>

  <!--
    jackson-databind 2.21.4: CVE-2026-54515 (MEDIUM 5.3).
    Decision: NO FIX + ACCEPTED.
    Reason:   Already at the version the Spring Boot 3.5.16 parent BOM manages; no
              patched release is available to upgrade to. Score 5.3 is below the
              build's CVSS >= 7 gate, so it does not fail the build. Recorded here so
              the report stays clean AND the acceptance is revisited rather than
              forgotten.
    Assumes:  no jackson-databind patch > 2.21.4 addressing this appears. The <until>
              forces this back into the report for review; re-check then and upgrade
              if a fix exists.
    Reviewed: Jason McKay, 2026-07-24
  -->
  <suppress until="2027-01-24Z">
    <filePath regex="true">.*[/\\]jackson-databind-.*\.jar</filePath>
    <cve>CVE-2026-54515</cve>
  </suppress>

</suppressions>
